- `defines(v1, ...)`: Range only lists `"Delivery Crew picks-up items at the event site"`, omitting the `"Customer returns the items to the store"` option explicitly stated in the source.
- `guard(b1, n3, ...)`: Specifies a condition only for the delivery crew; missing a guard for the `"Customer returns the items to the store"` alternative, violating the described two-path choice.
- `guard(b2, ...)`: Defines guards for missing and damaged statuses but omits a guard for `"Order is complete and all items were returned"`, leaving the third branch option unconditioned despite `precedence(b2, c1)`.
- `defines(v3, ...)`: Range only lists `"Customer does not return the missing items"`, omitting `"Customer returns the missing items"` as stated in the source.
- `branch(b3)`: Lacks any `guard` statements for its outgoing edges, failing to model the conditional continuation for both returning and not returning missing items.
- `guard(b4, n10, ...)`: Only guards the repaired path; missing a guard for the `"Items can't be repaired and are discarded"` condition on the `b4` → `n11` edge.
- `defines(v5, ...)`: Range only lists `"Items are stored for future orders"`, omitting `"Items are packed for an existing order"` as stated in the source.
- `precedence(b5, n14)` & `branch(b5)`: Defines only one outgoing edge, forcing all converged flows into `action(n14)` and ignoring the alternative storage path; incorrectly mandates `"Email customer with replacement charges"` after the packing/storing decision, whereas the source ties this email to item damage/discards or serves as a final concluding step.
- `action(n6, "Customer returns the missing items?")`, `action(n9, "Items repair status?")`, `action(n13, "Items packed for an existing order?")`: Modeled as executable steps rather than decision queries, and their immediate successor branches (`b3`, `b4`, `b5`) lack the required guards to faithfully capture the conditional routing described in the text.

Structural Check
- [S1] STC9 failed: Each branch node must have exactly one incoming and at least two outgoing edges. | Node `b1` has in=1 and out=1. | Node `b3` has in=1 and out=1. | Node `b5` has in=1 and out=1. | DSL: branch(b1,XOR); branch(b3,XOR); branch(b5,XOR)
- [S2] STC11 failed: Each variable definition must have a valid range with at least two distinct values. | Variable `v1` has an invalid range `['Delivery Crew picks-up items at the event site']`. | Variable `v3` has an invalid range `['Customer does not return the missing items']`. | Variable `v5` has an invalid range `['Items are stored for future orders']`.
- [S3] STC15 failed: Every variable used in a guard must be formally defined. | Guard `v1 = 'Delivery Crew picks-up items at the event site'` on `b1 -> n3` uses undefined variable `v1`. | DSL: guard(b1,n3,"v1 = 'Delivery Crew picks-up items at the event site'")
- [S4] STC16 failed: Every outgoing edge of XOR/OR branches must have exactly one guard. | XOR/OR edge `b2 -> c1` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b3 -> c1` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b4 -> n11` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b5 -> n14` has 0 guards; exactly one is required.

Logical Sanity Check
- [L1] LSC1 failed: Every guard must be satisfiable under the declared ranges of its referenced variables. | Guard `v1 = 'Delivery Crew picks-up items at the event site'` on `b1 -> n3` cannot be evaluated: `v1` has no declared range. | DSL: guard(b1,n3,"v1 = 'Delivery Crew picks-up items at the event site'")
- [L2] LSC3 failed: Guards outgoing from XOR/OR branches must cover the whole evaluated state space. | Branch `b1` cannot be evaluated: `v1` has no declared range. | Branch `b2` has no enabled outgoing guard at state [v2=Complete and all items were returned]. | Branch `b4` has no enabled outgoing guard at state [v4=Items can't be repaired and are discarded].
